<h2>A steady, short-transit Gulf market</h2>
<p>Oman takes a consistent flow of Indian banana, mango, maize and fish meal, benefiting from a short 4–7 day transit to Sohar or Muscat that keeps fresh produce viable on this route without the tighter cold-chain margins longer transits demand.</p>

<h2>Salalah's regional role</h2>
<p>Beyond serving Oman's own market, the port of Salalah in the south operates as a transshipment point reaching further into East Africa and the wider Indian Ocean region. If your buyer's business extends beyond Oman itself, it's worth asking whether Salalah routing serves their broader distribution needs.</p>

<h2>Straightforward Gulf trade conditions</h2>
<p>With the Omani Rial pegged to the US dollar, currency risk on this lane is minimal, and documentation requirements are generally in line with typical Gulf practice — phytosanitary certification, certificate of origin, and halal certification where the product or buyer requires it.</p>

